# GRADUATED from `known_gaps/` (R44 Track K) — the DICT comprehension's
# VALUE channel.
#
# AXIS CELL: comp-kind = dict `{k: v ...}` · source = `Vector[int]` · result
# = `int` key -> `String` VALUE.
#
# ⚠ THE VALUE CHANNEL IS THE HALF A KEY-ONLY FIX MISSES. The key is
# derivable from the source element, so a fix that threads only the key looks
# complete and leaves this cell wrong. The dict mint therefore carries TWO
# element slots and closes only when BOTH are known.
#
# RED-VERIFIED (self-host lane, pre-fix driver): `2` then a RAW POINTER
# printed as an integer, at rc 0 — a `String` value stored in an 8-byte slot
# and read back as a number.
#
# ⚠ THE RULE ITS `known_gaps` HEADER STATED WAS MEASURABLY TOO NARROW, and
# correcting it is part of the graduation. That header said the self-host
# set/dict arms are "WRONG iff any RESULT-CHANNEL element is not `int`". A
# set comprehension over a STRING SOURCE with EVERY result channel `int`
# (`{c.len() for c in s0}`) is ALSO wrong at HEAD — it prints an EMPTY
# collection at rc 0 — because the element-type helper early-returned empty
# for a String source and the comprehension then lowered NO LOOP AT ALL. No
# grid in the round sampled a set or dict over a String source, so nothing
# falsified the rule; the falsifiers now ship beside this file as
# `set_strsrc_allint` and `dict_strsrc_allint`. The real rule is: the mint
# was wrong whenever the accumulator's element type could not be recovered
# from the DESTINATION, which the result element now supplies directly.
#
# FIXTURE PLACEMENT: subdirectory (see any NET A cell's header for the full
# reasoning). LANE: self-host only — the Rust lane was already correct, so a
# `run_gg`-wired body would be GREEN ON ARRIVAL and pin nothing. STAGE: 0.
# RUNG: 1a (both real lanes agree post-fix; the longhand statement form of
# each program is the held-fixed control and agrees on both lanes).

from std.collections import Dict

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int n0():
    return 3

int n1():
    return 4

String tag(int n):
    if n == 3:
        return mk("thr", "ee")
    return mk("fo", "ur")

void main():
    Vector[int] nums = [n0(), n1()]
    Dict[int, String] d = {n: tag(n) for n in nums}
    print(d.len())
    print(d[3])
